          lemma: "λίθος ἐπὶ λίθον … τῆς σῆς παρουσίας",
          title: "Two questions braided — the key to the whole discourse",
          body: "The disciples are showing off the architecture, and with reason: Herod's temple was among the wonders of the world — courses of stone forty feet long, gold plate that blinded in the morning sun; the rabbis said whoever had not seen it had never seen a beautiful building. Jesus' reply — **not one stone on another** — was fulfilled with terrible literalness in AD 70, when Titus's legions burned and then dismantled the complex (the retaining courses of the platform, today's Western Wall, are what remained *below* the buildings). But the key to everything that follows is the disciples' **double question** on the Mount of Olives (Zechariah's mountain of the end, again): *when will these things be* — the temple's fall — *and what is the sign of your **παρουσία** and the completion of the age?* In their minds these were one event: the temple falling could only mean the world ending. The whole discourse is Jesus carefully **unbraiding** what they braided — some of it answers the first question (fulfilled, datable, within that generation), some the second (undatable, thief-in-the-night), and the discourse's difficulty for every later reader is that the two threads alternate without labels. Παρουσία, note, is not ”return” but a state-visit word: the arrival of a king in his city. Keep the two questions in separate hands, and the chapter largely sorts itself; fuse them, and you get two millennia of failed date-charts — which is, as it happens, the exact practice the discourse itself repeatedly forbids.",
