      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a24.36.1',
          kind: "text",
          refs: ['g24.34.9', 'g24.36.17'],
          lemma: "ἡ γενεὰ αὕτη … οὐδὲ ὁ Υἱός",
          title: "“This generation” and “not even the Son” — the two verses that police each other",
          body: "Here stand, two verses apart, the discourse's hardest claim and its deepest restraint — and each disciplines the other. **Verse 34**: *this generation will not pass until all these things happen* — the third of Matthew's hard clocks (10:23; 16:28). If ”all these things” means the temple's destruction and its attendant horrors (the disciples' *first* question), the sentence was precisely fulfilled: Jerusalem fell in 70, within forty years, one biblical generation. If it means the παρουσία too, the readings multiply (γενεά as ”this people,” or the generation that sees the final signs) — and honesty requires saying the natural reading is the first, which is also the one that lets the verse be simply *true*. Then **verse 36**, one of the most remarkable sentences in the New Testament: of that day and hour no one knows, not the angels, **οὐδὲ ὁ Υἱός** — *not even the Son*. The early church found this so uncomfortable that some copyists deleted the phrase (it is textually secure); no community invents ignorance for its Lord — the saying survives because he said it, and it is among the strongest authenticity-marks in the gospels. It is also the charter of every Christian's proper agnosticism about dates: if the Son declined to know, every prediction chart ever drawn was contradicting him by existing. The two verses together give the discourse its final shape: about the fall of the temple, *you can know — watch the fig tree*; about the end, *you cannot — watch yourself*. What follows (Noah, thief, servants) is entirely ethics of the unknowable date.",
        },
      ],
