          lemma: "παραλαμβάνεται … γρηγορεῖτε … τὴν τροφὴν ἐν καιρῷ",
          title: "One taken, one left — and what “watching” actually means",
          body: "Two clarifications this passage has long needed. First, ”one taken, one left” — pressed into service as the great rapture proof-text, filmed and novelized as the righteous vanishing from cars and cockpits. Read it inside its own analogy: in the days of Noah, the flood came and **took them all away** — the ones *taken* (ἦρεν) are the swept-off, the destroyed; Noah, the saved man, is the one *left*. On the passage's own logic being ”taken” is what you don't want, or at minimum the point is not the direction of removal at all but the **cut through every ordinary pair** — same field, same mill, same work, different readiness; the division runs invisibly through adjacent lives, and no one can tell from outside which is which. Second, ”watch” (**γρηγορεῖτε**). Every apocalyptic movement has read it as *scan the skies* — dates, signs, bunkers. The parable that closes the chapter defines it otherwise, and the definition is almost comically domestic: the watching servant is the one the master finds **giving the household their food at the proper time**. Not staring at the horizon: feeding people. The wicked slave's failure likewise has nothing to do with eschatology and everything to do with ethics — ”my lord delays” becomes a licence to beat the staff and drink. Readiness, in Jesus' own gloss, is not prediction but *faithfulness that doesn't depend on the schedule*: live so that the timing of the audit is a matter of indifference. The person doing their assigned work needs no advance notice — which is precisely why none is given.",
